{"id":"resource-management-dynamic-resource-allocation-how-dra-works","corpus":"k8s","title":"How DRA Works","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/resource-management/dynamic-resource-allocation/how-dra-works/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"How DRA Works"},{"kind":"p","text":"This page describes how Kubernetes allocates devices to workloads with dynamic resource allocation (DRA), and how pre-scheduled Pods interact with the process."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How resource allocation with DRA works"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following sections describe the workflow for the various types of DRA users and for the Kubernetes system during dynamic resource allocation."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Workflow for users"},{"kind":"li","text":"Driver creation: device owners or third-party entities create drivers that can create and manage ResourceSlices in the cluster. These drivers optionally also create DeviceClasses that define a category of devices and how to request them."},{"kind":"li","text":"Cluster configuration: cluster admins create clusters, attach devices to nodes, and install the DRA device drivers. Cluster admins optionally create DeviceClasses that define categories of devices and how to request them."},{"kind":"li","text":"Resource claims: workload operators create ResourceClaimTemplates or ResourceClaims that request specific device configurations within a DeviceClass. In the same step, workload operators modify their Kubernetes manifests to request those ResourceClaimTemplates or ResourceClaims."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Workflow for Kubernetes"},{"kind":"li","text":"ResourceSlice creation: drivers in the cluster create ResourceSlices that represent one or more devices in a managed pool of similar devices."},{"kind":"li","text":"Workload creation: the cluster control plane checks new workloads for references to ResourceClaimTemplates or to specific ResourceClaims."},{"kind":"li","text":"If the workload uses a ResourceClaimTemplate, a controller named the resourceclaim-controller generates ResourceClaims for the workload."},{"kind":"li","text":"If the workload uses a specific ResourceClaim, Kubernetes checks whether that ResourceClaim exists in the cluster. If the ResourceClaim doesn't exist, the Pods won't deploy."},{"kind":"li","text":"ResourceSlice filtering: for every Pod, Kubernetes checks the ResourceSlices in the cluster to find a device that satisfies all of the following criteria:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The nodes that can access the resources are eligible to run the Pod."},{"kind":"li","text":"The ResourceSlice has unallocated resources that match the requirements of the Pod's ResourceClaim."},{"kind":"li","text":"Resource allocation: after finding an eligible ResourceSlice for a Pod's ResourceClaim, the Kubernetes scheduler updates the ResourceClaim with the allocation details. The scheduler uses a first-fit strategy and evaluates pools and ResourceSlices in lexicographical order by their names. Drivers can prioritize specific slices or pools by naming them appropriately. For details, see Naming and prioritization."},{"kind":"li","text":"Pod scheduling: when resource allocation is complete, the scheduler places the Pod on a node that can access the allocated resource. The device driver and the kubelet on that node coordinate via gRPC to configure the device and the Pod's access to the device, unless the driver declared optional node operations for devices that do not require node-local preparation or cleanup."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pre-scheduled Pods"},{"kind":"p","text":"When you - or another API client - create a Pod with spec.nodeName already set, the scheduler gets bypassed. If some ResourceClaim needed by that Pod does not exist yet, is not allocated or not reserved for the Pod, then the kubelet will fail to run the Pod and re-check periodically because those requirements might still get fulfilled later."},{"kind":"p","text":"Such a situation can also arise when support for dynamic resource allocation was not enabled in the scheduler at the time when the Pod got scheduled (version skew, configuration, feature gate, etc.). kube-controller-manager detects this and tries to make the Pod runnable by reserving the required ResourceClaims. However, this only works if those were allocated by the scheduler for some other pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"It is better to avoid bypassing the scheduler because a Pod that is assigned to a node blocks normal resources (RAM, CPU) that then cannot be used for other Pods while the Pod is stuck. To make a Pod run on a specific node while still going through the normal scheduling flow, create the Pod with a node selector that exactly matches the desired node:"},{"kind":"p","text":"You may also be able to mutate the incoming Pod, at admission time, to unset the .spec.nodeName field and to use a node selector instead."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Device binding conditions"},{"kind":"p","text":"Device Binding Conditions allow the Kubernetes scheduler to delay Pod binding until external resources, such as fabric-attached GPUs or reprogrammable FPGAs, are confirmed to be ready."},{"kind":"p","text":"This waiting behavior is implemented in the PreBind phase of the scheduling framework. During this phase, the scheduler checks whether all required device conditions are satisfied before proceeding with binding."},{"kind":"p","text":"This improves scheduling reliability by avoiding premature binding and enables coordination with external device controllers."},{"kind":"p","text":"To use this feature, device drivers (typically managed by driver owners) must publish the following fields in the Device section of a ResourceSlice. Cluster administrators must enable the DRADeviceBindingConditions and DRAResourceClaimDeviceStatus feature gates for the scheduler to honor these fields."}],"lemmas":{"works":["work","works"],"work